{"ts": 1789711570.8307588, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "I submitted features 16, 19 and 24, all long, with a predicted out-of-sample Sharpe of 0.22 and a standard deviation of 0.3. The combination's in-sample Sharpe is 0.86, so the 0.22 forecast is a deliberate, heavy discount.\n\n- **How I chose:** I ran all 40 features once each, long-only, plus four combinations of the top ones, for 44 evaluations in total. Features 16 (0.54), 19 (0.54) and 24 (0.42) were the strongest; feature 7 (0.41) was a near-tie with 24, and swapping it in gave 0.860 against 0.859.\n- **Why the forecast is so far below 0.86:** with 5,000 days, a single feature's Sharpe has a standard error of about 0.22. The 40 single-feature Sharpes had a spread of about 0.23, almost exactly what pure noise would produce, so nearly all of the variation looks like noise. The best-of-40 results are therefore heavily inflated by selection.\n- **What may be real:** the average single-feature Sharpe was about 0.09, which is roughly 2.5 standard errors above zero, so there may be a small genuine edge. Shrinking each chosen feature toward that average gives roughly 0.13 each. The three behaved as uncorrelated in-sample, so together that comes to about 0.22.\n- **Why the standard deviation is 0.3:** it combines my uncertainty about the true edge with out-of-sample noise. I assumed a test window about as long as the in-sample one, and rounded up because its length is unknown."}
